===Etymology 2===
Of {{der|en|gmq|-}} origin, such as {{der|en|da|tang}} or {{der|en|sv|tång}}, from {{der|en|non|þang|þongull, þang}}.  See also {{cog|no|tongul}}, {{cog|fo|tongul}}, {{cog|is|þöngull}}.

{{rfi|en}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|seaweed}} Any large type of [[seaweed]], especially a [[species]] of {{taxfmt|Laminaria|genus}}.
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|X||[…] if with thee the roaring wells<br>⁠Should gulf him fathom-deep in brine;<br>⁠And hands so often clasp’d in mine,<br>Should toss with '''tangle''' and with shells.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1917|editor=Kenneth Macleod|chapter=The Road to the Isles|title=Songs of the Hebrides
|passage=You've never smelled the '''tangle''' o' the Isles.}}
# {{lb|en|in the plural}} An instrument consisting essentially of an iron bar to which are attached swabs, or bundles of frayed rope, or other similar substances, used to capture starfishes, sea urchins, and other similar creatures living at the bottom of the sea.
# {{lb|en|Scotland}} Any long hanging thing, even a lanky person.

